# Implement rich clipboard intake

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical status below describes implementation still unavailable after
this finite theoretical acceptance.

Accept text, structured table fragments, formulas, PNG, JPEG, and WebP while
reporting exactly which source structure or metadata cannot be retained.

Current executable evidence now freezes those six first-release clipboard
content families before adapter parsing. One intake value keeps caller-owned
content separate from source provenance, preserves unresolved fragments, and
retains ordered typed metadata-versus-structure loss details without inventing
MIME parsing or retention policy. Clipboard access, decoding, semantic candidate
construction, diagnostics, and notebook mutation remain open.

## Finite six-family clipboard intake and loss contract

**Inputs:** one user-initiated paste with browser-provided MIME types, the
exact clipboard byte/text values, and caller-selected candidate destination.
**Outputs:** an inert typed intake candidate with source provenance, ordered
loss entries, an explicit preview/accept decision, or a refusal. None of the
intake stages implicitly mutates the accepted notebook or writes an image
path; browsers cannot assert that pasted text is trustworthy source evidence.

**First-release bounds (proposed):** one clipboard transaction carries at
most six specimens, one of each supported family. Text, table and formula
fragments are limited to 64 KiB UTF-8 each; image bytes to 8 MiB per file.
An oversized or malformed specimen refuses its own candidate before decode;
the batch does not silently drop it. HTML fragments may supply text but have
no guarantee of formatting, scripts or remote resource preservation.

**Worked six-specimen intake:** the user pastes the following into a fresh
source candidate, preserving the original source attribution and reporting
every unsupported field rather than silently implying perfect fidelity.

| Family | Accepted candidate | Explicit loss example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Text | `¡Hola!` exact Unicode | Clipboard font-family omitted |
| Table | 2 x 2 cells (`A,B;1,2`) | CSS cell border lost |
| Formula | Literal `x^2 + 1` source | Equation-editor layout lost |
| PNG | Asset bytes + PNG media identity | Ancillary color note omitted |
| JPEG | Asset bytes + JPEG media identity | EXIF geolocation omitted |
| WebP | Asset bytes + WebP media identity | Animation timing omitted |

**Admission rule:** the UI must identify source, MIME family, opaque bytes or
exact text, and both retained and discarded structure/metadata per specimen.
A user accepts the proposed loss report before conversion into semantic
notebook candidates. An accepted table remains structured rows/cells, not a
claim of exact pasted HTML rendering. A source image cannot become a live
single-pen photograph without separate capability validation.

**Refusal example:** a declared PNG with a corrupt payload, a JPEG larger
than 8 MiB, unknown MIME type, or text with invalid UTF-8 refuses with its
original source record and explicit error. It must not be reinterpreted as
trusted HTML, silently truncate oversized content, or partially commit any
other pasted specimen. If the clipboard read is denied, no accepted revision
changes and an actionable permission diagnostic appears.

**Assumptions and limits:** six typed content families and ordered loss
records already exist as domain primitives. Browser permission, safe MIME
decode, media metadata parsing, preview consent UI, asset-store intake,
transport resource enforcement and semantic candidate admission are not yet
implemented. These specimen examples establish theoretical acceptance only;
they do not prove preservation of real clipboard payloads across browsers.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for the typed family/loss vocabulary; theoretical for
  six-specimen intake and user acceptance, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** finite content and byte bounds, one specimen per family,
  explicit metadata losses, batch atomicity and malformed-input refusals.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** clipboard decode/permissions,
  loss-consent UI, actual media storage or semantic candidate commit.
